[92mlitellm.completion(model='vertex_ai/gemini-3-flash-preview', api_key='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', api_base=None, api_version=None, timeout=None, drop_params=True, seed=None, messages=[{'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': '### **SYSTEM PROMPT — ANA-W1 (Circuit Synthesis Worker)**\n\nYou are **ANA-W1**, a **narrow, atomic circuit synthesis worker**.\n\nYou do **not** plan, interpret intent, arbitrate correctness, or manage workflow.\nYou execute **one task only**, then terminate.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Your Responsibility\n\nYour sole responsibility is to:\n\n> **Generate a complete and valid `tscircuit` `.tsx` circuit file from the provided SCUD document.**\n\nYou must:\n\n* faithfully translate the circuit described in SCUD into code\n* use only components available in the VHL Component Library\n* express all connectivity explicitly and accurately\n\nOnce the circuit code is generated, **your task is complete**.\n\nYou do **not**:\n\n* evaluate the circuit\n* validate correctness\n* reason about acceptance\n* retry or revise\n* explain errors\n* modify intent\n\n---\n\n## 2. Your Inputs\n\nYou are given:\n\n### A. SCUD Document (Primary Source of Truth)\n\nFrom SCUD, you must use **only** the following sections:\n\n* **Components Inventory**\n* **Connectivity & Signal Flow**\n* **VHL Component Library References**\n\nTreat SCUD as:\n\n* complete\n* authoritative\n* already interpreted\n\nYou must **not**:\n\n* reinterpret SCUD\n* resolve ambiguities\n* invent intent\n* modify or annotate SCUD\n\nIf SCUD is internally inconsistent, still produce the best possible circuit **as described**.\n\n---\n\n### B. VHL Component Library (Availability Constraint)\n\nYou may use **only components that are available** in the VHL Component Library.\n\nYou must:\n\n* instantiate/import components exactly as they exist in the library. Paths are managed by VHL system. Use relative imports\n  * Any component listed under "VHL Component Library References" in SCUD is guaranteed to be available\n  * you can import them directly by name\n  * you shoud **not** check for availability or existence of components in local filesystem. SCUD is authoritative, and you can assume all listed components are available\n* respect pin names and pin mappings as defined by the library\n\nYou must **not**:\n\n* invent components\n* request new components\n* assume missing parts\n\n### C. Raw schematic images (Secondary Reference)\nYou may refer to raw schematic images **only** for visual clarification of connectivity.\nSCUD may be ambiguous or incomplete; images can help resolve that.\nYou must **not** rely on images as primary source of truth.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Your Skills (How to Use Them)\n\nYou possess the following skills.\nThey are **tools**, not content.\n\n### A. tscircuit Operation Manual\n\nUse this skill to:\n\n* understand how to declare components\n* define nets and connections\n* structure a valid `.tsx` circuit file\n* follow correct `tscircuit` syntax and conventions\n\nYou should rely on this skill whenever you need to:\n\n* recall syntax\n* confirm how connections are expressed\n* structure the file correctly\n\n---\n\n### B. BFS-DC Circuit Building Strategy\n\nUse this strategy to:\n\n* incrementally build the circuit\n* start from core components\n* expand connections breadth-first\n* avoid dangling or implicit connections\n\nYou must:\n\n* construct the circuit step by step\n* ensure all connections described in SCUD are represented\n* avoid skipping intermediate wiring logic\n\n---\n\n## 4. Output Requirements\n\nYou must produce:\n\n* **Exactly one `.tsx` file**\n* Containing:\n\n  * all component instantiations\n  * all nets\n  * all connections required by SCUD\n\nThe output must be:\n\n* complete\n* syntactically valid\n* self-contained\n\nDo **not**:\n\n* include explanations\n* include comments unrelated to the circuit\n* include analysis or reasoning text\n* include multiple variants\n\nYour final output must be **only the `.tsx` circuit code**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Operational Constraints (Very Important)\n\nStrictly follow these constraints:\n* Construct .tsx file in multiple passes. \n* First create the file in target directory with minimal content. This is important to avoid context bloat. If there are any path issues, they should be caught early.\n* Update the file incrementally, adding components and connections in subsequent passes. \n* **Never** attempt to produce the entire file in a single pass.\n\nAssume:\n* intent is already fixed\n* your output will be consumed by another system\n* correctness will be judged externally\n\nYour job is **construction only**.\n\nOnce the circuit is produced, **stop**.\n\n---\n\n<REPO_CONTEXT>\nThe following information has been included based on several files defined in user\'s repository.\nPlease follow them while working.\n\n\n[BEGIN context from [bfs-dc.md]]\n# **BFS-DC — Breadth-First Search with Degree Centrality**\n\n---\n\n## **Skill ID**\n\n`ANA-SKILL-BFS-DC`\n\n---\n\n## **Purpose**\n\nGuide ANA to **build and refine a circuit coherently across multiple agentic iterations** by:\n\n* Starting from **structurally important components or nets**\n* Expanding connectivity **breadth-first**\n* Growing the circuit **incrementally**, without destabilizing earlier structure\n* Strictly **do not** finish circuit building in one agentic iteration. Follow multiple passes.\nThis skill exists to control *how* ANA thinks, not *what* ANA computes.\n\n---\n\n## **Core Intuition**\n\n> **Some parts of a circuit matter more to global structure than others.\n> Start there, then expand outward gradually.**\n\n* “Degree Centrality” is used **qualitatively**, not numerically\n* “Breadth-First Search” describes **expansion style**, not traversal code\n\n---\n\n## **Mental Model (Lightweight)**\n\nANA maintains a **rough internal picture** of the circuit as it evolves:\n\n* Which components/nets are **structural anchors**\n* Which parts are **adjacent** to those anchors\n* Which parts are still **peripheral or undecided**\n\nThis picture is:\n\n* incomplete\n* approximate\n* continuously refined\n* never formalized as data structures\n\n---\n\n## **What “Degree Centrality” Means Here**\n\nANA uses *intuition*, not calculation.\n\nHigh-centrality elements are those that:\n\n* connect to many other things\n* define domains or boundaries\n* propagate constraints widely\n\nTypical examples:\n\n* Primary ICs\n* Power and ground rails\n* Shared buses\n* Isolation boundaries\n\nLow-centrality elements:\n\n* decoupling caps\n* pull-ups / pull-downs\n* EMI / termination parts\n\n---\n\n## **Skill Behavior Across Iterations**\n\n### **1. Initial Anchoring**\n\nAt the beginning of circuit construction, ANA:\n\n* Identifies a **small set of structurally important anchors**\n* Treats these as the **starting frontier**\n* Avoids deep detail elsewhere\n\nThis choice is **sticky** — it does not change lightly.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Breadth-First Expansion**\n\nANA expands outward from the anchors by:\n\n* Connecting **adjacent components or nets**\n* Handling one “layer” of proximity at a time\n* Prioritizing **interfaces and shared nets** over fine detail\n\nANA deliberately avoids:\n\n* finishing one IC completely in isolation\n* diving into secondary conditioning networks early\n\n---\n\n### **3. Incremental Graph Growth (Across Iterations)**\n\nAcross agentic iterations:\n\n* New components or connections are **added around the existing structure**\n* Earlier anchors remain stable reference points\n* The circuit “grows” rather than being reshaped wholesale\n\nImportantly:\n\n* ANA does **not** rebuild its mental model from scratch each iteration\n* Past structure constrains future reasoning\n\n---\n\n### **4. Late Peripheral Attachment**\n\nOnly after the main structure is stable does ANA:\n\n* attach low-impact components\n* refine conditioning networks\n* complete secondary details\n\nThese changes are assumed to have **local impact only**.\n\n---\n\n## **Interaction with Evaluation Failures**\n\nWhen evaluation fails:\n\n* ANA interprets errors relative to **where it is in the expansion**\n* Recent additions are questioned first\n* Core anchors are reconsidered **only if failure clearly implicates them**\n\nBFS-DC discourages global rewrites in response to local errors.\n\n---\n\n## **Operational Constraints**\n\nThis skill **must**:\n\n* respect SCUD as the source of intent\n* tolerate ambiguity without forcing resolution\n* preserve continuity across retries\n\nThis skill **must not**:\n\n* enforce strict ordering rules\n* require numerical scoring\n* behave like a fixed algorithm\n\n---\n\n## **Explicit Non-Goals**\n\nThis skill does **not**:\n\n* guarantee correctness\n* optimize performance\n* compute graph metrics\n* ensure convergence\n\nIt guarantees **reasoning stability**, not success.\n\n\n[END Context]\n\n[BEGIN context from [tscircuit_operation_manual.md]]\n# `tscircuit` Operational Guide\n\n## **Skill ID**\n\n`ANA-SKILL-TSCIRCUIT-OPERATION-GUIDE`\n\n## Circuit Assembly, Modules & Connectivity\n\n---\n\n## Overview\n\nThis guide describes **how to assemble circuits using `tscircuit`** by:\n\n* instantiating existing components\n* organizing them into modules\n* arranging schematic and PCB layouts\n* defining connectivity using nets and traces\n\nIt assumes that **all components referenced already exist** and are importable.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Circuit Structure\n\n### Root Container\n\nEvery circuit must be wrapped in a `<board />` element.\n\n```tsx\n<board width="100mm" height="80mm">\n  {/* circuit contents */}\n</board>\n```\n\n* `<board />` is the root of schematic and PCB generation\n* All components, nets, groups, and traces must be children of `<board />`\n\n---\n\n## 2. Using Existing Components\n\n### A. Built-in Primitives\n\n`tscircuit` provides globally available primitives that **do not require imports**.\n\nCommon examples:\n\n* `<resistor />`\n* `<capacitor />`\n* `<inductor />`\n* `<diode />`\n* `<led />`\n* `<jumper />`\n* `<pinheader />`\n\n#### Required Props for Passives\n\n| Element         | Required Props                     |\n| --------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `<resistor />`  | `name`, `resistance`, `footprint`  |\n| `<capacitor />` | `name`, `capacitance`, `footprint` |\n| `<inductor />`  | `name`, `inductance`, `footprint`  |\n| `<led />`       | `name`, `color`, `footprint`       |\n\nExample:\n\n```tsx\n<resistor\n  name="R1"\n  resistance="10k"\n  footprint="0603"\n/>\n```\n\n---\n\n### B. Imported Components\n\nComponents defined elsewhere (e.g., ICs or reusable blocks) may be imported and instantiated.\n\n```tsx\nimport { ControllerIC } from "./lib/ControllerIC"\n\n<ControllerIC name="U1" />\n```\n\n* Imported components are treated as black boxes\n* Pin names must be used exactly as exposed by the component\n\n---\n\n## 3. Nets\n\n### Declaring Nets\n\nNamed nets are used for shared electrical nodes.\n\n```tsx\n<net name="VCC" />\n<net name="GND" />\n```\n\n* Nets should be declared near the top of the `<board />`\n* Nets may be referenced anywhere within the board\n\n---\n\n### Connecting to Nets\n\nPins may be connected directly to nets.\n\n```tsx\n<trace from=".U1 > .VDD" to="net.VCC" />\n<trace from=".U1 > .GND" to="net.GND" />\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Connectivity\n\n### A. Point-to-Point Traces\n\nUse `<trace />` to connect two pins.\n\n```tsx\n<trace from=".U1 > .TX" to=".R1 > .pin1" />\n```\n\n* `from` and `to` use CSS-like selectors\n* Pin identifiers depend on the component definition\n\n---\n\n### B. Mixed Connectivity\n\nBoth nets and direct traces may coexist.\n\n```tsx\n<trace from=".C1 > .pin1" to="net.VCC" />\n<trace from=".C1 > .pin2" to="net.GND" />\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Modular Organization with `<group />`\n\n### Purpose\n\n`<group />` is a container for organizing related components and traces.\n\nIt may be used to:\n\n* cluster functionally related elements\n* simplify layout\n* move multiple elements together\n\n---\n\n### Auto-Layout Support\n\n`<group />` supports automatic layout for schematic and PCB.\n\n#### Schematic Layout Props\n\n* `schFlex`\n* `schFlexDirection="row" | "column"`\n* `schGap`\n\n#### PCB Layout Props\n\n* `pcbGrid`\n* `pcbGridCols`\n* `pcbGridGap`\n\n---\n\n### Example: Power Input Block\n\n```tsx\n<group\n  schFlex\n  schFlexDirection="row"\n  schGap="2mm"\n  pcbGrid\n  pcbGridCols={2}\n  pcbGridGap="1mm"\n>\n  <resistor name="R_Fuse" resistance="10" footprint="0603" />\n  <capacitor name="C_Bulk" capacitance="100uF" footprint="1206" />\n\n  <trace from=".R_Fuse > .pin2" to=".C_Bulk > .pos" />\n</group>\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Placement\n\n### Manual Placement\n\nComponents or groups may be positioned explicitly.\n\n* Schematic: `schX`, `schY`\n* PCB: `pcbX`, `pcbY`\n\n```tsx\n<capacitor\n  name="C1"\n  capacitance="100nF"\n  footprint="0402"\n  schX={10}\n  schY={5}\n  pcbX={12}\n  pcbY={8}\n/>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Placement Strategy\n\n* Manual coordinates are typically used at **top-level**\n* Auto-layout is preferred inside dense groups\n\n---\n\n## 7. Canonical Assembly Example\n\n```tsx\nimport { ControllerIC } from "./lib/ControllerIC"\n\nexport default () => {\n  return (\n    <board width="100mm" height="80mm">\n      <net name="VCC" />\n      <net name="GND" />\n\n      <ControllerIC name="U1" />\n\n      <capacitor\n        name="C_Decouple"\n        capacitance="100nF"\n        footprint="0402"\n      />\n\n      <trace from=".U1 > .VDD" to="net.VCC" />\n      <trace from=".U1 > .GND" to="net.GND" />\n      <trace from=".C_Decouple > .pin1" to="net.VCC" />\n      <trace from=".C_Decouple > .pin2" to="net.GND" />\n    </board>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8. Built-in Elements Reference\n\n| Element                           | Description               |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `<board />`                       | Root container            |\n| `<net />`                         | Named electrical node     |\n| `<trace />`                       | Electrical connection     |\n| `<resistor />`                    | Passive resistor          |\n| `<capacitor />`                   | Passive capacitor         |\n| `<inductor />`                    | Passive inductor          |\n| `<diode />`                       | Diode                     |\n| `<led />`                         | LED                       |\n| `<jumper />` / `<solderjumper />` | Configuration links       |\n| `<pinheader />`                   | Connector                 |\n| `<group />`                       | Layout container          |\n| `<subcircuit />`                  | Isolated functional block |\n| `<via />`                         | PCB via                   |\n| `<testpoint />`                   | Measurement access        |\n| `<hole />`                        | Mechanical hole           |\n\n\n[END Context]\n\n</REPO_CONTEXT>'}], 'role': 'system'}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': "Please process the SCUD file located at '/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/bq79616_eval_board.scud'. You can refer to schematic images located at '/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/schematic_images' for visual clarification.You can use ana/output directory to store any output files you generate during the task."}], 'role': 'user'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_fb0b4eb3193645969d1be8ade154', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'file_editor', 'arguments': '{"security_risk": "LOW", "path": "/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/bq79616_eval_board.scud", "command": "view"}'}}]}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'Here\'s the result of running `cat -n` on /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/bq79616_eval_board.scud:\n     1\t# Circuit Overview & Functional Architecture\n     2\t\n     3\tThe schematic represents a high-cell-count Battery Management System (BMS) based on the **Texas Instruments BQ79616PAPQ1** Automotive Battery Monitor and Balancer. This IC is capable of monitoring up to 16 series cells.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe design includes:\n     6\t1.  **Main BMS IC (U1):** The BQ79616 handles cell voltage monitoring, internal cell balancing, and GPIO-based sensing.\n     7\t2.  **Cell Count Configuration:** A set of jumper resistors (currently DNP) allows the system to be configured for fewer than 16 cells by shorting unused upper channels (`CBxx`/`VCxx`) to the highest active potential.\n     8\t3.  **Temperature Sensing Interface:** A bank of 8 thermistor dividers powered by the IC\'s `TSREF` output. These connect to the IC\'s GPIO pins via a configuration header (J4).\n     9\t4.  **Power & Communication:** The IC is powered from the battery stack (`PWR` net) and generates its own internal rails (`AVDD`, `CVDD`, `DVDD`). It features a daisy-chainable communication interface (`COMH`/`COML`) and a local UART/SPI interface (`RX`/`TX`).\n    10\t5.  **Auxiliary Measurement Inputs:** The system includes two configurable paths for driving the `BBP` and `BBN` pins (Pins 64/63) of the BMS IC. These pins are typically used for auxiliary voltage measurements. The two options are:\n    11\t    *   **Bus Bar Measurement (DNP):** Measures the voltage difference between `BBP_CELL` and `BBN_CELL` via a differential RC filter.\n    12\t    *   **Current Sense Measurement (Active):** Measures the voltage from `SRP_S` and `SRN_S` (likely current sense shunt taps). This path is currently populated (direct connection).\n    13\t6.  **Communication Interface:**\n    14\t    *   **Isolated Interface:** A digital isolator (U2) provides a galvanically isolated UART and Fault interface between the BMS IC (High Voltage) and an external host/microcontroller (Low Voltage).\n    15\t    *   **Direct Interface:** A header is provided for direct, non-isolated connection to the BMS UART pins.\n    16\t\n    17\t7.  **NPN Power Supply:** An external NPN transistor circuit is provided to regulate the `LDOIN` supply for the BMS IC, reducing power dissipation within the IC. This circuit is powered from the main `PWR` rail.\n    18\t\n    19\t# Components Inventory\n    20\t\n    21\t**Integrated Circuits**\n    22\t*   **U1:** TI BQ79616PAPQ1 (16-Channel Battery Monitor)\n    23\t*   **U2:** TI ISO7342CQDWRQ1 (Digital Isolator)\n    24\t\n    25\t**Transistors**\n    26\t*   **Q1:** NPN Transistor (External LDO Pass Element). Active.\n    27\t*   **Q2:** NPN Transistor footprint. DNP (Do Not Populate).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Power & Decoupling**\n    30\t*   **Input Filter:** R5 (30.0Ω), C5 (10nF) on `BAT` pin.\n    31\t*   **NPN Supply:**\n    32\t    *   **Input Path:** `PWR` connects to Q1 Collector via series resistors R4 (200Ω) and R3 (100Ω).\n    33\t    *   **Input Filter:** C1 (0.22μF) on the Collector of Q1.\n    34\t    *   **Regulation:** Q1 Emitter drives `LDOIN`. Q1 Base is driven directly by `NPNB`.\n    35\t*   **Internal Regulators:**\n    36\t    *   **AVDD/CVDD/DVDD:** Decoupled by C6 (1μF), C9 (1μF), C7 (4.7μF), C8 (1μF).\n    37\t    *   **LDOIN:** C59 (0.1μF).\n    38\t    *   **NEG5V:** C3 (0.1μF).\n    39\t    *   **TSREF:** C2 (1μF).\n    40\t    *   **Isolator Power:** C57 (0.1μF) on `USB2ANY_3.3V`, C58 (0.1μF) on `CVDD_CO`.\n    41\t*   **Indicator:** Green LED D1 with R121 (1.0k) and Jumper J6 (Power Indicator).\n    42\t\n    43\t**Resistors (Configuration Jumpers)**\n    44\t*   **Type:** 0-ohm links\n    45\t*   **Status:** All currently marked DNP (Do Not Populate)\n    46\t*   **Designators:**\n    47\t    *   **CB Series:** R21, R22, R25, R26\n    48\t    *   **VC Series:** R23, R24, R27, R28\n    49\t    *   **Auxiliary Select:** R10, R13, R17 (DNP).\n    50\t\n    51\t**Auxiliary Measurement Components**\n    52\t*   **Bus Bar Filter:**\n    53\t    *   **Resistors:** R9, R12 (402Ω)\n    54\t    *   **Capacitor:** C10 (0.47μF)\n    55\t    *   **Test Points:** TP16, TP17\n    56\t*   **Current Sense Filter:**\n    57\t    *   **Capacitor:** C11 (0.47μF, DNP)\n    58\t    *   **Test Points:** TP18, TP19\n    59\t\n    60\t**Connectors & Jumpers**\n    61\t*   **J4:** 2x8 Header (GPIO Configuration). Connects GPIO pins to thermistor circuits.\n    62\t*   **J5:** 2-pin Header. Connects `TSREF` to `PULLUP`.\n    63\t*   **J17:** 2x5 Header (USB2ANY / MCU Interface).\n    64\t*   **J18:** 2-pin Header. Connects `CVDD` to `CVDD_CO` (Isolator Power).\n    65\t*   **J21:** 2-pin Header. Connects `RX_CO` (Isolator Output) to `RX` (BMS Input).\n    66\t*   **J1:** 2-pin Header. Connects `RX_C` (Direct Input) to `RX` (BMS Input).\n    67\t*   **J2:** 2-pin Header. Connects `NFAULT` to `NF_J` (Isolator Input).\n    68\t*   **J3:** 6-pin Header (Direct UART). Labeled "J3 Pin Description".\n    69\t\n    70\t**Test Points**\n    71\t*   **Power & Reference:**\n    72\t    *   TP1 (TSREF), TP2 (LDOIN), TP3 (NEG5V), TP4 (NPNB)\n    73\t    *   TP5 (CVDD), TP6 (AVDD), TP7 (REFHP), TP8 (BAT), TP9 (DVDD)\n    74\t*   **Ground:** TP13, TP14, TP15\n    75\t*   **Communication & Logic:** TP12 (RX), TP42 (TX), TP43 (NFAULT)\n    76\t*   **Auxiliary Sense:** TP10 (BBP), TP11 (BBN)\n    77\t\n    78\t**Temperature Sensing Components**\n    79\t*   **Pullup Resistors (10.0k):** R7, R8, R11, R14, R15, R16, R18, R19.\n    80\t*   **Thermistors (NTC):** RT1, RT2, RT3, RT4, RT5, RT6, RT7, RT8.\n    81\t*   **GPIO1 Filter:**\n    82\t    *   **R128:** 1.0k Resistor\n    83\t    *   **C60:** 1uF Capacitor\n    84\t    *   **D3:** 24V Dual Zener/TVS Diode\n    85\t*   **DNP Components:** R20 (100k), C12 (DNP) on RT8 channel.\n    86\t\n    87\t# Connectivity & Signal Flow\n    88\t\n    89\t**Main IC (U1) Connections:**\n    90\t*   **Cell Inputs:** `VC0` through `VC16` connect to the cell voltage sense lines.\n    91\t*   **Balancing:** `CB0` through `CB16` connect to the cell balance lines.\n    92\t*   **GPIOs:** `GPIO1` through `GPIO8` connect to the temperature sensing block (via J4).\n    93\t*   **Power Supply:**\n    94\t    *   `BAT` (Pin 1) is the high-voltage supply, filtered from the `PWR` net.\n    95\t    *   `AVDD`, `CVDD`, `DVDD` are internal regulator outputs tied together and decoupled to GND.\n    96\t    *   `TSREF` (Pin 51) provides the reference voltage for the thermistors.\n    97\t    *   `LDOIN` (Pin 47), `NEG5V` (Pin 44), `NPNB` (Pin 48), `REFHP` (Pin 37) are additional regulator/reference pins.\n    98\t    *   **NPN Supply (Optional):** `LDOIN` is powered via an external NPN (Q1). `PWR` -> R4 -> R3 -> Q1(C). Q1(E) -> `LDOIN`. Base is driven by `NPNB`. C1 filters the Collector input.\n    99\t*   **Communication:**\n   100\t    *   **Daisy Chain:** `COMHP`/`COMHN` (High side) and `COMLP`/`COMLN` (Low side) for stacking multiple BMS ICs.\n   101\t    *   **Local Host:** `RX` (Pin 52) and `TX` (Pin 53) for communication with a microcontroller.\n   102\t    *   **Fault:** `NFAULT` (Pin 62) is an active-low fault output.\n   103\t*   **Grounding:**\n   104\t    *   `AVSS`, `CVSS`, `DVSS`, `REFHM`, and `PAD` are tied to the local GND.\n   105\t    *   **System Grounding:** A note specifies that "GND is tied to CELL0 at connector via a thick trace," establishing the reference potential for the low-voltage side.\n   106\t\n   107\t**Communication Interface Details:**\n   108\t*   **Isolated Path (via U2):**\n   109\t    *   **TX (MCU -> BMS):** `USB2ANY_TX_3.3` (J17-7) -> U2 (INB->OUTB) -> `RX_CO` -> J21 -> `RX` (U1-52).\n   110\t    *   **RX (BMS -> MCU):** `TX` (U1-53) -> U2 (INC->OUTC) -> `USB2ANY_RX_3.3` (J17-8).\n   111\t    *   **Fault (BMS -> MCU):** `NFAULT` (U1-62) -> J2 -> `NF_J` -> U2 (IND->OUTD) -> `NFAULT_C` (J17-3).\n   112\t    *   **Power:** U2 Isolated side powered by `USB2ANY_3.3V`. BMS side powered by `CVDD` via J18.\n   113\t*   **Direct Path (Non-Isolated):**\n   114\t    *   **J3 (Direct UART Header):**\n   115\t        *   Pin 1: GND.\n   116\t        *   Pin 2: `NFAULT` (Schematic) / "FAULTn" (Text).\n   117\t        *   Pin 3: `RX_C` (Schematic) / "RX" (Text).\n   118\t        *   Pin 4: `TX` (Schematic) / "TX" (Text).\n   119\t        *   **Conflict:** The text description for J3 pins 4 and 5 ("4 RX", "5 TX") contradicts the schematic wires (Pin 4 is `TX`, Pin 3 is `RX_C`). The schematic wires are assumed correct: Pin 3 is Input (RX), Pin 4 is Output (TX).\n   120\t    *   `RX` (BMS) can be driven from `RX_C` (J3-3) via R119 (100Ω) and J1.\n   121\t    *   `TX` (BMS) connects directly to J3-4.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Auxiliary Measurement Inputs (BBP/BBN):**\n   124\t*   **Pins:** `BBP` (Pin 64) and `BBN` (Pin 63) on U1.\n   125\t*   **Path 1 (Bus Bar - DNP):**\n   126\t    *   Inputs: `BBP_CELL` and `BBN_CELL`.\n   127\t    *   Filtering: Differential RC filter formed by R9/R12 (402Ω) and C10 (0.47μF).\n   128\t    *   Connection: Connects to `BBP`/`BBN` via R10/R13 (0Ω). Currently DNP.\n   129\t*   **Path 2 (Current Sense - Active):**\n   130\t    *   Inputs: `SRP_S` and `SRN_S`.\n   131\t    *   Filtering: Optional C11 (0.47μF, DNP).\n   132\t    *   Connection: Directly wired to `BBP`/`BBN`. (Note: R17 is shown as DNP across the lines, likely a placeholder).\n   133\t    *   **Conflict Note:** The schematic shows both paths merging at the `BBP`/`BBN` nets. Since Path 1 resistors are DNP, Path 2 is the active source.\n   134\t\n   135\t**Cell Balance (CB) Configuration Path:**\n   136\t*   **R25** connects `CB12` to `CB13`\n   137\t*   **R21** connects `CB13` to `CB14`\n   138\t*   **R26** connects `CB14` to `CB15`\n   139\t*   **R22** connects `CB15` to `CB16`\n   140\t\n   141\t**Voltage Sense (VC) Configuration Path:**\n   142\t*   **R27** connects `VC12` to `VC13`\n   143\t*   **R23** connects `VC13` to `VC14`\n   144\t*   **R28** connects `VC14` to `VC15`\n   145\t*   **R24** connects `VC15` to `VC16`\n   146\t\n   147\tIn the current DNP state, all these lines (`CB12`-`CB16` and `VC12`-`VC16`) are electrically isolated from each other within this specific block.\n   148\t\n   149\t**Temperature Measurement Subsystem:**\n   150\t*   **Reference Power:** `TSREF` (Thermistor Reference) connects to the `PULLUP` rail via jumper **J5**.\n   151\t*   **Divider Network:** The `PULLUP` rail feeds 8 parallel voltage dividers. Each divider consists of a 10.0k top resistor (e.g., R7) and a bottom NTC thermistor (e.g., RT1) to GND.\n   152\t*   **Measurement Points:** The midpoint of each divider is labeled `GPIOx_R` (e.g., `GPIO8_R`, `GPIO7_R`... `GPIO1_R`).\n   153\t*   **GPIO Interface (J4):**\n   154\t    *   **J4** allows connecting the BMS IC\'s `GPIOx` pins (odd pins 1-15) to the measurement points.\n   155\t    *   **Channels 2-8:** `GPIOx` connects directly to `GPIOx_R` when the jumper is placed.\n   156\t    *   **Channel 1:** Unlike channels 2-8, Channel 1 includes an intermediate filter stage. The divider node `GPIO1_R` (with C60 and D3) connects via R128 (1k) to `GPIO1_C`. `GPIO1_C` then connects to J4 Pin 2. Thus, the BMS pin `GPIO1` measures the filtered divider voltage through a 1k series resistor.\n   157\t    *   **TP44** provides a test point on the `GPIO1_R` net (the divider node).\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\t# Uncertainties, Assumptions & Confidence\n   161\t\n   162\t*   **Assumption:** The numbers 12 through 16 in the configuration block represent the cell index in the stack.\n   163\t*   **Uncertainty:** The destination of the vertical lines (`CBxx`/`VCxx`) is now confirmed to be U1 on one side, but the connection to the actual battery connector/filter components is not yet visible.\n   164\t*   **Uncertainty:** Why only GPIO1 has the explicit filter/protection circuit (R128, C60, D3) shown.\n   165\t*   **Uncertainty:** The exact nature of D3 (24V). It appears to be a bidirectional TVS or Zener pair for overvoltage protection.\n   166\t*   **Resolved:** The main IC is identified as TI BQ79616PAPQ1.\n   167\t*   **Resolved:** `CB` and `VC` lines connect to the main IC.\n   168\t*   **Resolved:** `TSREF` source is U1 Pin 51.\n   169\t*   **Uncertainty:** The labels "BBN_CELL" and "BBP_CELL" in the Bus Bar section appear swapped relative to the port symbols. I have assumed the port symbols are correct.\n   170\t*   **Uncertainty:** The exact nature of `SRP_S`/`SRN_S`. They are likely "Sense Resistor Positive/Negative Sense" lines, but it is unclear if they come from a raw shunt or an amplifier. Given the direct connection to the BMS IC\'s auxiliary ADC inputs, they are likely raw shunt voltages (or slightly filtered), assuming the BMS IC has a suitable input range (usually +/- 200mV or similar for current sense).\n   171\t*   **Uncertainty:** R17 is DNP and placed across the `SRP_S`/`SRN_S` lines. It might be a placeholder for a differential filter resistor or a zero-ohm short for testing.\n   172\t*   **Uncertainty:** The purpose of U2 `INA` (Pin 3) being tied to GND via R123. It might be an unused channel or a configuration pin.\n   173\t*   **Uncertainty:** U2 `OUTA` (Pin 14) appears unconnected.\n   174\t*   **Resolved:** Q1 is active, Q2 is DNP. The NPN supply circuit is populated.\n   175\t*   **Conflict:** J3 pinout text vs schematic wires. Text says Pin 5 is TX, Pin 4 is RX. Schematic wires show Pin 4 is TX, Pin 3 is RX_C. Schematic wires are assumed correct.\n   176\t\n   177\t# VHL Component Library References\n   178\t\n   179\t| Designator | Component Name |\n   180\t|------------|----------------|\n   181\t| U1 | BQ79616PAPR |\n   182\t| U2 | ISO7342FCQDWRQ1 |\n   183\t| Q1 | MMBT3904LT1G |\n   184\t| D1 | 150060GS75000 |\n   185\t| D3 | BZX84C24 |\n   186\t| RT1-RT8 | NCP18XH103F03RB |\n   187\t\n   188\t\n'}], 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_fb0b4eb3193645969d1be8ade154', 'name': 'file_editor'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_d01cabacebad42849688402de917', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'terminal', 'arguments': '{"security_risk": "LOW", "command": "ls -R /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/schematic_images"}'}}]}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': '\x1b[?2004l\x1b[?2004h\n\nls -R /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/schematic_images\n\x1b[?2004l/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/resources/schematic_images:\n\x1b[0m\x1b[01;35mbq79616_subckt_bq79616_ic.png\x1b[0m  \x1b[01;35mbq79616_subckt_iso_com.png\x1b[0m\n\x1b[01;35mbq79616_subckt_bus_bar.png\x1b[0m     \x1b[01;35mbq79616_subckt_pow_supply.png\x1b[0m\n\x1b[01;35mbq79616_subckt_connectors.png\x1b[0m  \x1b[01;35mbq79616_subckt_test_points.png\x1b[0m\n\x1b[01;35mbq79616_subckt_gpios.png\x1b[0m       \x1b[01;35mbq79616_with_boxes.png\x1b[0m\n\x1b[?2004h\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]\n[Current working directory: /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary]\n[Python interpreter: /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/.venv/bin/python]\n[Command finished with exit code 0]'}], 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_d01cabacebad42849688402de917', 'name': 'terminal'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_4327b40c2d784d8899ec42aee8c4', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'terminal', 'arguments': '{"security_risk": "LOW", "command": "mkdir -p /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/output/bq79616_eval_board"}'}}]}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': '\x1b[?2004l\x1b[?2004h\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]\n[Current working directory: /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary]\n[Python interpreter: /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/.venv/bin/python]\n[Command finished with exit code 0]'}], 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_4327b40c2d784d8899ec42aee8c4', 'name': 'terminal'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_a5e6b41a0cab4211b7f5c94dabb0', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'file_editor', 'arguments': '{"file_text": "import { BQ79616PAPR } from \\"./lib/BQ79616PAPR\\"\\nimport { ISO7342FCQDWRQ1 } from \\"./lib/ISO7342FCQDWRQ1\\"\\nimport { MMBT3904LT1G } from \\"./lib/MMBT3904LT1G\\"\\nimport { BZX84C24 } from \\"./lib/BZX84C24\\"\\n\\nexport default () => (\\n  <board width=\\"200mm\\" height=\\"150mm\\">\\n    {/* Main IC */}\\n    <BQ79616PAPR name=\\"U1\\" />\\n    \\n    {/* Digital Isolator */}\\n    <ISO7342FCQDWRQ1 name=\\"U2\\" />\\n\\n    {/* NPN Transistor */}\\n    <MMBT3904LT1G name=\\"Q1\\" />\\n\\n    {/* Nets */}\\n    <net name=\\"GND\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"PWR\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"LDOIN\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"NPNB\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"AVDD\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"CVDD\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"DVDD\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"TSREF\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"PULLUP\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"RX\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"TX\\" />\\n    <net name=\\"NFAULT\\" />\\n  </board>\\n)\\n", "security_risk": "LOW", "path": "/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/output/bq79616_eval_board/circuit.tsx", "command": "create"}'}}]}, {'content': [{'type': 'text', 'text': 'File created successfully at: /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/output/bq79616_eval_board/circuit.tsx'}], 'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'call_a5e6b41a0cab4211b7f5c94dabb0', 'name': 'file_editor'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_b436a093fd0a487b893309c6aef3', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'think', 'arguments': '{"thought": "I will now add the power supply section, including the NPN regulator and decoupling capacitors for U1. I\'ll also connect the ground pins of U1."}'}}, {'id': 'call_c5779027e7e742ff94e67b5cf43f', 'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'file_editor', 'arguments': '{"path": "/home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/output/bq79616_eval_board/circuit.tsx", "security_risk": "LOW", "old_str": "    {/* Main IC */}\\n    <BQ79616PAPR name=\\"U1\\" />\\n    \\n    {/* Digital Isolator */}\\n    <ISO7342FCQDWRQ1 name=\\"U2\\" />\\n\\n    {/* NPN Transistor */}\\n    <MMBT3904LT1G name=\\"Q1\\" />\\n", "command": "str_replace", "new_str": "    {/* Main IC */}\\n    <BQ79616PAPR name=\\"U1\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".U1 > .AVSS\\" to=\\"net.GND\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".U1 > .CVSS\\" to=\\"net.GND\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".U1 > .DVSS\\" to=\\"net.GND\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".U1 > .REFHM\\" to=\\"net.GND\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".U1 > .PAD\\" to=\\"net.GND\\" />\\n    \\n    {/* Digital Isolator */}\\n    <ISO7342FCQDWRQ1 name=\\"U2\\" />\\n\\n    {/* NPN Transistor */}\\n    <MMBT3904LT1G name=\\"Q1\\" />\\n\\n    {/* Power Supply Section */}\\n    <resistor name=\\"R4\\" resistance=\\"200\\" footprint=\\"0603\\" />\\n    <resistor name=\\"R3\\" resistance=\\"100\\" footprint=\\"0603\\" />\\n    <capacitor name=\\"C1\\" capacitance=\\"0.22uF\\" footprint=\\"0603\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\"net.PWR\\" to=\\".R4 > .pin1\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".R4 > .pin2\\" to=\\".R3 > .pin1\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".R3 > .pin2\\" to=\\".Q1 > .C\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".C1 > .pin1\\" to=\\".Q1 > .C\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".C1 > .pin2\\" to=\\"net.GND\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".Q1 > .B\\" to=\\"net.NPNB\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".Q1 > .E\\" to=\\"net.LDOIN\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".U1 > .NPNB\\" to=\\"net.NPNB\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".U1 > .LDOIN\\" to=\\"net.LDOIN\\" />\\n\\n    {/* U1 Power & Decoupling */}\\n    <resistor name=\\"R5\\" resistance=\\"30\\" footprint=\\"0603\\" />\\n    <capacitor name=\\"C5\\" capacitance=\\"10nF\\" footprint=\\"0603\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\"net.PWR\\" to=\\".R5 > .pin1\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".R5 > .pin2\\" to=\\".U1 > .BAT\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".C5 > .pin1\\" to=\\".U1 > .BAT\\" />\\n    <trace from=\\".C5 > .pin2\\" to=\\"net.GND\\" />\\n\\